# Fetched module artifact — NEVER commit this.
# It is downloaded from S3 by ci/fetch-modules.sh at build time.
# The only record of which version was used is `moduleVersion` in each config.
modules/

# Downloaded module tarballs, cached by ci/lib/modules.sh so a re-run does not
# hit S3 again. Safe to delete at any time.
.build/
*.tar.gz

# docs/STACK-REGISTRY.md IS COMMITTED, and is deliberately NOT listed here.
#
# It is generated, but it is also the catalogue people read to answer "what is
# this stack called and what outputs can I consume?" - and a generated file
# nobody can browse on GitHub answers that for nobody.
#
# It stays honest without a build gate:
#   - ci/lint.sh REGENERATES it on every run, so anyone who lints picks the
#     change up and sees it in `git status`
#   - ci/buildspec.yml does NOT check it is current. It used to in the infra
#     repo, and that gate failed three pipeline runs over a one-line
#     documentation diff.
#
# A copy that is one commit behind is a readable catalogue. A gate that blocks
# deploys over a stale table is not worth having, and the check that actually
# matters - every Fn::GetStackOutput resolving - runs inside ci/lint.sh and
# never needed a committed file at all.

# NO GENERATED IAM TEMPLATES HERE YET.
#
# The infra repo ignores tier1-security/iam-policies/main.yaml and
# tier1-security/iam-roles/main.yaml, because those are built from its iam/
# folder by ci/gen-iam.py. This repo has neither. When app-scoped IAM roles
# arrive, add the generated paths here at the same time as gen-iam.py - a
# generated template that gets committed is a second place the same policy
# lives, which is a second place it can be wrong.

# cloudformation package output
packaged.yaml
*.packaged.yaml
*.packaged.json

# rendered parameter files
.params/
*.params.json

# local
.env
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
.code-workspace
